---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About Dr Henry Franks — Async Forest Advisory"
  description="Async Forest Advisory is Dr Henry Franks: Warwick PhD on trust in multi-agent systems, startup CTO and chief architect for over a decade, responsible-AI lecturer at KCL and Notre Dame."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container about">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h1>The practice is one person. That's the point.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          When you commission a report from Async Forest, you know exactly who
          read your code, whose judgement you're paying for, and who is
          answerable if the conclusion is wrong.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about__portrait" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="mono">Photo to follow</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2>Dr Henry Franks</h2>
      <p>
        I've spent my career on one question from two directions: <strong>how do
        you trust a system you didn't build and don't control?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        The academic direction first. My PhD at Warwick was on trust,
        reputation, and cooperation in open multi-agent systems — how
        independent, self-interested software agents can verify each other's
        behaviour when there's no central authority to appeal to. That work was
        published in the field's main journal and conference (AAMAS), and it
        turned out to be a strangely good apprenticeship for the current
        moment, when everyone's stack is filling up with autonomous systems
        nobody fully controls.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the practical direction: a decade and a half building the systems
        themselves. Founder and CTO of a London SaaS startup; data science and
        engineering in regulated insurance at Howden's technology arm; chief
        software officer at a US energy-tech startup; and since 2022, CTO and
        now Chief Architect at Climate Policy Radar, where we build open AI
        tools used for climate law and policy research worldwide. I've shipped
        machine learning to production in domains where being wrong is
        expensive, and I've been the person accountable when it broke.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside that, I lecture annually on responsible AI — the social,
        ethical, and legal side — at King's College London and the University
        of Notre Dame, and I've spoken on AI and climate at venues from COP to
        CIO summits.
      </p>
      <h3 class="record__heading">The short version</h3>
      <ul class="record">
        <li><strong>PhD, University of Warwick</strong> — trust and cooperation in open multi-agent systems; published in AAMAS and <em>Autonomous Agents and Multi-Agent Systems</em></li>
        <li><strong>Chief Architect, Climate Policy Radar</strong> — previously its CTO; AI systems for climate law and policy, used globally</li>
        <li><strong>15+ years in startups</strong> — founder/CTO, chief software officer, principal engineer, data scientist, across SaaS, insurance, and energy</li>
        <li><strong>Guest lecturer on responsible AI</strong> — King's College London and University of Notre Dame, annually</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <h2>Why independent assurance</h2>
      <p>
        Software teams have always marked their own homework; AI made the
        homework bigger and the marking automatic. Codebases are now part-written
        by tools with well-measured blind spots, demos are indistinguishable
        from products, and the person telling you "it's fine" is usually the
        person who built it — or a model summarising the model.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old answer to this problem still works: an independent expert with
        the right background reads the actual system and signs their name to a
        conclusion. My academic work was about making trust checkable in
        systems of autonomous agents. This practice is the same idea, applied
        commercially, one codebase at a time.
      </p>
      <p>
        I run it solo and deliberately small: fixed scopes, written
        deliverables, few meetings. You get depth and a straight answer; I get
        to do the kind of focused technical work I'm best at. Nobody gets a
        junior analyst with a checklist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <span class="eyebrow">Next step</span>
      <h2>See if the fit is right</h2>
      <p>
        Read the <a href="/services/" class="dark-link">three audits</a>, or
        just describe what you need checked and I'll answer plainly — including
        "I'm not the right person" when that's the truth.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn--on-dark">Start a conversation</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(240px, 320px);
    gap: var(--space-16);
    align-items: center;
  }
  .about__portrait {
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface-warm-2);
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    color: var(--text-subtle);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .record__heading { margin-top: var(--space-12); }
  .record { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1em; }
  .record li { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .dark-link { color: var(--gray-200); }
  .section--dark .btn { margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .about { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-8); }
    .about__portrait { max-width: 260px; }
  }
</style>
